{"id": "z1715", "pool": "easy-mathword", "n_q_chunks": 9, "onset_chunk": 7, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "He had 1 36 books initially, and after the sale, he had 2 7 left. So to find out how many he sold, we just subtract 2 7 from 1 36. That gives us 10 9. So Paul sold 109 books at the garage sale.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 20, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1723", "pool": "easy-mathword", "n_q_chunks": 14, "onset_chunk": 13, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So there were 1 06 dollars in Olivia's wallet. After she visited a supermarket and a showroom, there were 26 dollars left. If she spent 49 dollars at the showroom, how much did she spend at the supermarket? Let' s figure it out step by step. First, we add up what she spent in total. She started with 1 06 dollars and ended up with 2 6 dollars. That means she spent 106 minus 26, which is 80 dollars altogether. We know she spent 4 9 dollars at the showroom, so to find out what she spent at the supermarket, we subtract the 49 from the total of 80. That gives us 31 dollars. So, Olivia spent 31 dollars at the supermarket.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 42, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1731", "pool": "easy-mathword", "n_q_chunks": 10, "onset_chunk": 9, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Let's figure this out. First, add up all the bottles. There are 80 regular soda bottle s and 5 4 diet soda bottles. So, 8 0 plus 54 equal s 1 34 bottle s in total. Now, they had 36 apple s. To find out how many more bottles there were than apples, subtract the number of apple s from the total number of bottles. That' s 1 34 minus 36, which equal s 98. So, they had 98 more bottle s than apples.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 33, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1739", "pool": "easy-mathword", "n_q_chunks": 10, "onset_chunk": 10, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "To find out how much carpet Jes sie needs, we first figure out the area of one room. Each room is 19 feet long and 1 8 feet wide, so we multiply those numbers: 1 9 times 18 equal s 3 42 square feet per room. Since she has 20 rooms in total, we multi ply the area of one room by the number of rooms: 3 42 times 20 equals 6 8 40 square feet. So, Jesse needs 6 8 4 0 square feet of carpet to cover all her rooms.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 35, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1747", "pool": "easy-mathword", "n_q_chunks": 13, "onset_chunk": 13, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "During summer break, 22,9 58 kids went to camp and 776 22 stayed home. To find out how many more stayed home, subtract the number who went to camp from those who stayed home: 77 62 2 minus 22, 958 equal s 5 4,66 4. So, 5 4,6 64 more kids stayed home than went to camp.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 19, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1755", "pool": "easy-mathword", "n_q_chunks": 8, "onset_chunk": 8, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Olivia spent 7 8 dollars and has 33 dollars left. So to find out how much she had at first, we just add those two amounts together. That means 78 plus 33 equal s 1 1 1 dollars. So O livia initially had 1 11 dollars.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 20, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1763", "pool": "easy-mathword", "n_q_chunks": 12, "onset_chunk": 11, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So if there are 3,13 4 73 kids in Lawrence County and 27 48 65 stay home during summer break, we can find the number that go to camp by subtract ing. That' s 3, 13 47 3 minus 274 865. When you do that sub traction, you get 3 85 88. So 3 85 88 kids go to camp during the break.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 29, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1771", "pool": "easy-mathword", "n_q_chunks": 10, "onset_chunk": 10, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "She weighed 69 kilograms before starting to go jogging every day. After losing 3 5 kilogram s, we can find her new weight by subtracting the weight lost from the original weight. So, 6 9 minus 3 5 equal s 34. Therefore, Je ssie now weighs 34 kilograms.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 23, "carrier_ended": null}
