{"id": "z1795", "pool": "easy-mathword", "n_q_chunks": 14, "onset_chunk": 13, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "There are 3 84 oranges in Philip's collection. If the bananas are organized into 2 3 45 groups, and orange s are organized into 16 groups, how big is each group of oranges? Well, to figure this out, we just need to divide the total number of oranges by the number of groups they're split into. So that would be 3 84 divided by 1 6. And when you do that calculation, you get 24. Each group of oranges contains 24 orange s.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 30, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1803", "pool": "easy-mathword", "n_q_chunks": 10, "onset_chunk": 9, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "There are 11 basket s of peaches. Each basket has ten red peaches and eighteen green peache s. How many peaches are in the baskets altogether?", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 9, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1811", "pool": "easy-mathword", "n_q_chunks": 10, "onset_chunk": 9, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "David did 5 6 more push-up s than Zachary, and he did 3 8 push-ups. So let's figure out how many Z achary did. If David did 38 and that was 56 more than Zachary, then Zachary must have done 3 8 minus 56. That would be negative 1 8, which doesn't make sense for push-ups. Wait, I got that backwards. It should be the other way around. David did more, so to find Zachary' s count, we subtract the difference from David' s total. So it' s 38 minus 56, which still gives a negative number. Hmm, that can't be right. Let me re calculate. If David did 56 more than Zachary, then Zachary did 38 minus 56? No, that' s not it. Actually, if David did 56 more, we add the difference to get to David' s total. So to find Zachary's, we take David's total and subtract the extra amount. So it' s 38 minus 56? That still isn' t working. Oh, I see now. If David did 5 6 more, then Zachary did 3 8 minus 5 6? No, that's wrong again. Let' s try this: If David did 56 more", "eot_seen": false, "n_ans_chunks": 60, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1819", "pool": "easy-mathword", "n_q_chunks": 11, "onset_chunk": 11, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "The Ferris wheel has 6 seats, and each seat can hold 14 people. So the total capacity is 6 times 14, which is 84 people. Right now, there are 16 people on the wheel. To find out how many more can join, we subtract 1 6 from 8 4. That gives us 6 8. So, 6 8 more people can join the wheel.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 23, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1827", "pool": "easy-mathword", "n_q_chunks": 12, "onset_chunk": 12, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Well, if the book had two chapters each with 4 05 pages, that means there were a total of 8 10 pages in the entire book. If Frank took 6 6 4 days to finish it all, then he read about 1.2 1 pages per day on average. Since he finished an entire book over such a long period, it's unlikely he read more than one chapter daily, so he probably read less than one chapter per day, maybe just a few pages here and there.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 28, "carrier_ended": null}
