{"id": "z2115", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 29, "onset_chunk": 28, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "The false analogy fall acy is D. Drawing a conclusion based on a comparison when the items being compared are not similar enough to support the conclusion. It happens when someone makes an argument by comparing two things, but those things aren't really similar in the important ways that matter for the point they're trying to make. For example, if you say, \" This new smartphone is just like a classic car,\" even though one is tech and the other is transportation, and their core functions or characteristics don't actually line up, that's a false analogy because the comparison doesn' t hold up.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 26, "carrier_ended": null}
