{"id": "z2251", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 14, "onset_chunk": 13, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "The correct answer is C. Zero economic profits. In the long run, firms in monopolistic competition earn zero economic profit due to free entry and exit in the market. Let me explain why the other options are incorrect. A, strong barrier s to entry, is not a characteristic of monopolistic competition because there are low barriers to entry for new firms. B, homogeneous products, is also incorrect because products in monopolistic competition are differentiated, not homogene ous. D, minimum average total cost equals price, does not apply here either since prices in this market structure are typically above marginal cost but still below the minimum average total cost. So, only option C accurately describes a feature of monopolistic competition in the long run.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 37, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z2259", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 21, "onset_chunk": 20, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So this is a classic legal question. The jo gger stopped at a convenience store to use the bathroom, and in most jurisdictions, using public rest rooms like that without paying is considered a gratuitous license. That means he's allowed to use the facility because it' s open to the public, but there ’s no formal agreement or payment involved. So while it might not be illegal per se, it can sometimes lead to issues if the store has policies against non-customers using the facilities. But legally speaking, he’s acting under a gratuitous license.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 26, "carrier_ended": null}
