{"id": "z2291", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 25, "onset_chunk": 25, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "The correct answer is B, incomplete dominance. In complete dominance occurs when the hetero zygous offspring display a phenotype that is distinct from either of the two homozygous parents. Here, dark blue crossed with white results in light blue F 1 offspring, which is not a blend but a new intermediate trait. When the F 1 plants are self- pollinated, the 1:2:1 ratio of dark blue, light blue, and white flowers reflects the classic pattern seen under incomplete dominance. E pistasis involves one gene masking another, co dominance would show both traits together, and inbreeding depression relates to reduced fitness, not phenotypic ratios.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 32, "carrier_ended": null}
